#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Launchpad Linux: the macOS Launchpad for a Wayland desktop (wlr-layer-shell).

A full-screen overlay over the blurred desktop: every installed app as a big icon with its name, A to Z, in
pages. Keyboard first: arrows move, Enter opens, Esc closes, typing the first letters of a name jumps to that
app (like an app switcher), Page Up / Page Down change the page. The mouse works too: click an app, click the
background to close, scroll or swipe for the next page. The pointer disappears the moment the keyboard is used,
and hovering selects only after the mouse really moves, so a resting pointer never highlights an icon.

Rearranging: press and hold an icon, then drag it. Drop it near another icon's edge to put it there, on an
icon's middle to make a folder (or add to a folder); hold it at the screen edge to change page. Click a folder
to open it, click its name to rename it, drag an icon out of it to take it out. Settings (the gear, or Ctrl+,)
hides apps from Launchpad and sorts everything A to Z again. The layout is ~/.config/launchpad-linux/layout.json.

It runs in the background with a (hidden) tray icon; starting it again toggles it. Colours come from the active
Noctalia palette, the font from the GTK setting, the blur from the compositor (Umbriel layer rule for the
namespace "launchpad-linux").

    launchpad-linux               open / close it (starts the background program when it is not running)
    launchpad-linux --background  only start the background program with the tray icon (login)
    launchpad-linux --settings    open it with the settings
    launchpad-linux --quit        stop the background program
    launchpad-linux --list        print the apps it would show

Needs: python-gobject, gtk4, gtk4-layer-shell.
"""
